<article class="env-lemma" id="00OS">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00OS">Lemma <span data-tag="00OS">10.114.4</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $S$ be a finite type $k$-algebra which is an integral domain. Then $\dim (S) = \dim (S_{\mathfrak m})$ for any maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ of $S$. In words: every maximal chain of primes has length equal to the dimension of $S$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Write $S = k[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/\mathfrak p$. By Proposition <a href="/tag/00OQ" data-tag="00OQ">10.114.2</a> and Lemma <a href="/tag/00OR" data-tag="00OR">10.114.3</a> all the maximal chains of primes in $S$ (which necessarily end with a maximal ideal) have length $n - \text{height}(\mathfrak p)$. Thus this number is the dimension of $S$ and of $S_{\mathfrak m}$ for any maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ of $S$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>